WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.260
Before netrin had a name, a worm held the clue.

2
00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:09.500
How can one cue send cells in two directions?

3
00:00:10.633 --> 00:00:13.593
Start on the spinal cord map, then switch to the worm.

4
00:00:14.753 --> 00:00:17.133
There, UNC-6 is made on the ventral side.

5
00:00:18.117 --> 00:00:20.177
The worm C. elegans is transparent.

6
00:00:21.197 --> 00:00:24.717
Many of its cells can be named and found again in every animal.

7
00:00:25.717 --> 00:00:31.517
In 1990, Edward Hedgecock and colleagues followed migrating cells and pioneer axons.

8
00:00:32.517 --> 00:00:36.117
Pioneer axons are the first axons to lay down a route.

9
00:00:38.117 --> 00:00:40.397
Some cells move toward the ventral side.

10
00:00:41.497 --> 00:00:42.497
Others move away from it.

11
00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:45.300
The team looked at mutants of three genes.

12
00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:48.880
The first was unc-6, the gene for the cue itself.

13
00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:55.430
Lose the cue. Which migrations should fail: toward the ventral side, away from it,

14
00:00:55.430 --> 00:00:56.430
or both?

15
00:01:01.883 --> 00:01:08.043
Both. Without UNC-6, many migrations in both directions went the wrong way.

16
00:01:08.903 --> 00:01:11.623
The cells and axons still moved, but misdirected.

17
00:01:15.166 --> 00:01:19.366
Mutants of unc-40 failed mainly in movement toward the ventral side.

18
00:01:20.326 --> 00:01:22.046
Movement away was largely normal.

19
00:01:23.066 --> 00:01:25.546
Mutants of unc-5 showed the mirror image.

20
00:01:26.466 --> 00:01:28.046
They failed mainly in movement away.

21
00:01:29.066 --> 00:01:31.386
One cue, two readers, two directions.

22
00:01:32.426 --> 00:01:33.426
unc-40 reads toward.

23
00:01:34.186 --> 00:01:35.346
unc-5 reads away.

24
00:01:36.766 --> 00:01:41.726
In 1992, UNC-6 turned out to be a laminin-related protein.

25
00:01:42.906 --> 00:01:45.186
UNC-5 turned out to span the membrane.

26
00:01:46.266 --> 00:01:48.166
The table links genes to directions.

27
00:01:49.126 --> 00:01:51.926
To test the link, change the reader and leave the cue alone.

28
00:01:52.966 --> 00:01:56.566
Worm touch neurons do not normally carry UNC-5.

29
00:01:57.426 --> 00:01:59.266
Their axons do not grow dorsally.

30
00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:02.250
Force them to make UNC-5.

31
00:02:03.170 --> 00:02:04.610
Which way do their axons grow?

32
00:02:05.470 --> 00:02:07.770
And does the new route still need UNC-6?

33
00:02:13.783 --> 00:02:17.263
In 1993, Michel Hamelin and colleagues did this.

34
00:02:18.023 --> 00:02:21.723
The axons grew dorsally, away from the ventral UNC-6.

35
00:02:22.716 --> 00:02:25.136
And the new route still needed UNC-6.

36
00:02:29.266 --> 00:02:30.626
The cue never changed.

37
00:02:31.446 --> 00:02:32.906
It marked the ventral side throughout.

38
00:02:33.826 --> 00:02:35.126
The receptor set the direction.

39
00:02:36.716 --> 00:02:41.076
The takeaway: changing the receptor changed the answer to an unchanged cue.
